> Avoid overflowing a reciprocal when the quotient is finite

Computing an unscaled1/D before multiplying can overflow for a subnormal D even
when N/D is finite. In particular the smallest positive subnormal divided by
itself is1. The corrected producer also writes abs(N)=m_N·2^e_N, then forms
ldexp(sign·m_N·x,e_N−e_D). This preserves the quotient’s scale without
materializing an overflowing raw reciprocal. The control table includes equal
subnormals, minimum normal over minimum subnormal, equal maximum finite numbers,
signed underflow and a zero numerator.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/newton_division_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/newton_division.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    double tiny =
        std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(), normal =
        std::numeric_limits<double>::min(), large =
        std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
    std::vector<std::pair<double, double>> pairs{{tiny, tiny}, {normal, tiny},
                                                 {large, large}, {-tiny, large},
                                                 {0, -2}};
    std::vector<std::string> names{"sub/sub", "min/sub", "max/max", "underflow",
                                   "zero"};
    std::vector<double> updates;
    std::print("| control | actual quotient | built-in comparison | negative result sign | zero-numerator " "shortcut | underflowed | actual updates |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- | --- | ---: " "|\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < pairs.size(); ++i) {
        auto[n, d] = pairs[i];
        auto r = dm::algorithms::newton_division_run(n, d);
        updates.push_back(r.updates);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", names[i],
                   r.quotient, n / d, std::signbit(r.quotient), r.zero_shortcut,
                   r.underflowed, r.updates);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(updates, names, "actual updates");
    p.title("Floating-division scale controls: actual reciprocal work").x_label(
        "finite input control").y_label("actual reciprocal updates").save_svg(
        "build/newton_division_controls.svg");
}
```
